.7 points, Opus 5.5 answered E7 and lost 9.0, swinging Black's lead from -4.5 to +4.5. The engine preferred E8. White had a lead and handed it right back."}, {"turn": 28, "text": "The 12k's G4 had just lost 12.1 points, pushing Black's lead to -12.9. Opus 5.5 played H4 and lost 10.8 of that advantage, leaving the lead at -2.1. The engine's choice was F2."}, {"turn": 33, "text": "The 12k's C1 lost 28.6 points, the biggest single drop of the game, and Black's lead fell from -7.0 to -35.6. It put a Black stone in the corner beside White's C2 and C3 stones, and White suddenly stood far ahead."}, {"turn": 34, "text": "Opus 5.5 replied B2, which lost 29.6 points and returned the lead to -6.0. The engine wanted B8, up on the top left, and instead the edge fight went on."}, {"turn": 36, "text": "The same pattern repeated. After the 12k's B1 lost 28.2, Opus 5.5 played A2 and lost 28.8, taking the lead from -34.2 back to -5.5. The engine again preferred B8. Neither side managed to hold a big edge, and the game stayed within a few points."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "after.py places one move the player names and prints the resulting group liberties and captures, while count.py and libs.py only count territory and list groups on a pasted board; none of them loops over candidate moves or plays out continuations, so the player still picks every move itself.", "stop": false}}